+# Gödel-hole framing describes the boundary conditions selecting conscious loops
+
+Lines 18433‑18478 explain that Gödel holes are not a competing theory but a lens on boundary conditions: finite agents in open worlds face pockets they can’t fully model, forcing them to gate resources, maintain capacity limits, and spawn new representations. Conscious feel intensifies near these holes because the loop performs maximum meta-work (re-weighting, binding, re-writing). The framing clarifies why evolution converges on conscious-style controllers: the need to manage ever-emerging structural gaps.
+
