 # Minimal structural threshold arises from pointers, not external stimuli
 
-The smallest detectable difference (`Δ_min`) is not a fixed psychometric threshold but the smallest delta for which the agent's internal pointer/arisal system generates a new structural event. Track `Δ_min` as the infimum of deltas where `diff(x,y)` triggers a pointer (⇘) under the ontology's own metric, log it per-trial/context, and treat shifts in `Δ_min` as non-classical events (e.g., frame shifts or collapse-induced readiness). Experimental templates involve presenting nearly identical events, recording when the pointer fires, and logging the ledger entry, as AI_13 outlines.
+## Claim (formal)
+The minimal detectable difference Δ_min is defined by when the pointer/arisal system emits a new structural event; it is an internal threshold (Reach + diff-triggered) rather than an externally imposed psychometric value.
+
+## Philosophical Translation (of formal claim)
+“Smallest noticeable change” comes from the ontology’s own pointers reaching, not from imported lab thresholds.
+
+## Philosophical Justification
+Based on [[01_Statements/Definition/S-DF-pointer-structural]] and [[01_Statements/Definition/S-DF-difference-operator]], a new pointer event marks discriminability. Δ_min should therefore be the infimum Δ that triggers such a pointer under current resolution. Treating it as external would misalign with the reach/pointer ontology.
+
+## Clarifications / Further Context
+- Track Δ_min per context; shifts may indicate frame changes or collapse recoveries.
+- Avoid references to AI process logs; define measurement directly in terms of pointers/reach and diff.
*** End Patch
