 # Methodological roles — Doubting Subject (DS) and Relaxed Doubting Subject (RDS)
-## Content
-DS is a thought tool: a subject considered under maximal doubt that acknowledges only the structure of experiencing (not content). RDS relaxes DS just enough to follow reasoning and answer structural questions while still withholding content commitments.
+## Claim (formal)
+DS = a methodological subject under maximal doubt that acknowledges only experiential structure; RDS = a minimally relaxed DS that permits following derivations while still withholding content commitments.
+
+## Philosophical Translation (of formal claim)
+Use DS to strip assumptions to “what experiencing requires”; use RDS to cautiously allow reasoning steps without importing extra ontology.
+
+## Philosophical Justification
+To avoid smuggling metaphysics, early derivations adopt DS (experience + performative consistency, no content commitments). Some reasoning needs minimal relaxation (RDS) to track structural consequences without claiming more than warranted. These roles are scaffolding, not ontic additions, and should not leak into statement frontmatter beyond methodology.
 
 ## Implication
 These roles justify stripping imported assumptions early on and focusing on structural claims about experience/change. They are scaffolding for derivations, not ontological additions; canonical statements should not depend on DS/RDS beyond methodological framing.
*** End Patch
