 # Primitive — Bend metric (local deviation from self-continuation)
 ## Claim (formal)
 The bend τ of a segment quantifies deviation from its gauge‑aligned continuation. Given predicted continuation p(x) and realized y, τ := gauge_norm(p(x), y). Lower τ indicates coherent self‑continuation; higher τ indicates effort/curvature.
 
 ## Philosophical Translation (of formal claim)
 “Effort” is how much a trajectory must bend away from what it would have done to remain itself.
 
+## Philosophical Justification
+- [[S-DF-gauge-alignment-field]] sets the “straight ahead” direction in the current frame.
+- [[S-DF-localreach-topology]] and emergent metrics define comparability; bend is the residual when actual motion departs from gauge-aligned continuation.
+- Quantifying bend supplies a cost term for stabilization and routing; without it, “effort” is qualitative only.
+
+## Explanation (informal)
+Imagine where the pattern would go if it just kept being itself; measure how far the actual step diverges. That number is the bend, and it drives SE expenditure and curvature-aware planning.
+
+## Derivation (Philosophical)
+- Define predicted continuation via gauge; define norm via emergent metric; bend = norm(predicted, realized).
+- Accumulated bend along a path measures total deviation effort.
+
+## Derivation (Formal/Logical/Mathematical)
+```text
+τ(x→y) := || y - p(x) ||_Γ
+Path bend: τ_path := ⊕ τ_i over segments (monoidal sum).
+```
+
+## Proofs/Corollaries References
+- proof sketch: gauge provides a reference geodesic; bend is deviation from it.
+- corollary: used in [[S-DR-bend-metric-lawvere-attractors]], [[S-DF-ops-j1-bend-substitution]].
+
+## Clarifications / Further Context
+- Γ encodes gauge warp; choose norm consistent with [[S-DF-metric-space-emergent]].
+- High bend signals identity strain; can trigger SE accounting and gauge updates.
+
+## Next Steps in Chain
+- suggest: [[S-DR-bend-metric-lawvere-attractors]]
+- suggest: [[S-DF-ops-j1-bend-substitution]]
