+## Statement (corollary)
+In quantale logic, a proof corresponds to composing evidence along a path: A ⊗ B is the evidence for “go via A then B.” Choice/disjunction aggregates alternatives by min‑join ⊕ (keep the least‑cost path). Thus provability is reachability with minimal cost, and “or” means “take the easier path.”
+
+## Philosophical Translation
+To prove is to reach the target by chaining steps; “or” keeps the cheapest route.
+
+## Philosophical Justification
+Proof steps behave like path concatenation: order matters, cost adds, and weaker routes are dominated by stronger ones. Modeling this as a quantale preserves these intuitions and clarifies that classical proof search is a special case of path search.
+
+## Derivation (Formal/Logical/Mathematical)
+- From [[S-DR-quantale-evidence-composition]], ⊗ composes evidential costs via inf‑convolution.
+- The join ⊕ is defined as pointwise min, giving disjunction-as-best‑alternative.
+- Soundness: if each segment meets its requirement, their composition does; minimality follows from quantale monotonicity.
+
+## Proofs/Corollaries References
+- Boolean limit: [[S-DR-quantale-boolean-flattening-proof]] collapses graded paths to classical proof trees.
+- Residuation/implication: [[S-DR-quantale-residuation-implication]] characterizes “what remains to be shown.”
+
+## Clarifications / Further Context
+- Reachability applies to state graphs, program traces, and inference chains.
+- If costs are supports instead of penalties, swap order (max‑plus variant) but structure remains.
+
