 # Depth — minimal reach distance to Now
 ## Claim (formal)
 Depth(p; Now) := minimal length of Reach path from p to Now; provides a partial order for priors and supports neighborhood layering.
 
 ## Philosophical Translation (of formal claim)
 Some priors are “closer” than others — depth makes that precise without a clock.
 
+## Philosophical Justification
+- [[S-DF-reach-relation]] defines adjacency; layering those adjacencies produces a natural partial order without presuming external time.
+- Depth supplies a constructive notion of “recency/relevance” that feeds thresholds, SE windows, and auditability.
+- Without depth, priors cannot be prioritized coherently; with depth, we avoid arbitrary ordering that would break performative neutrality.
+
+## Derivation (Philosophical)
+- Choose LocalReach and allowable paths.
+- Define depth as the minimal path length/weight from p to Now within that constrained graph.
+- Show that this induces neighborhoods that respect the locality prior ([[S-DF-locality-prior]]).
+
+## Clarifications / Further Context
+- Depth is defined relative to a declared Reach metric (could be hop count, cost, or weighted by breath).
+- Depth is local and can change if LocalReach or Tx changes; report which configuration is used.
+- If multiple minimal paths exist, tie-break rules must be declared for deterministic use.
+
+## Next Steps in Chain
+- suggest: connect to [[S-DF-prm-depth-breadth-flip]] and [[S-CL-time-noise-thresholds]] for operational thresholds.
+
