 # Every action imprints structural footprints that bias future change
 
 A “jump,” “action,” or “event” is not consumed and forgotten; it leaves informational biases (memory traces) and structural shifts (environment/possibility rewrites) that persist in the field. These footprints alter the availability of future attractors, holes, or variables and make the field itself a recorder of its history. Account for both the informational residue and the structural deformities whenever you trace why a variable stabilized, collapsed, or invited new meta-dynamics, as described in AI_13 lines 3968‑3998.
+
+## Philosophical Justification
+- From dynamic-variable structure, actions modify both information and affordance geometry; ignoring footprints misstates later evolution.
+- Persistence of traces explains path dependence and why identical inputs can yield different futures after interventions.
+
+## Clarifications / Further Context
+- Footprints include memory traces, updated invariants, altered reachability/Tx admissibility.
+- Track both informational (weights, traces) and structural (topology, gauge) residues in audits.
+
+## Next Steps in Chain
+- suggest: integrate footprint logging into EC/RTV audits for variable stabilization/collapse.
*** End Patch
